  "difficulty_level": "H2",
  "difficulty_reason": "After the named VC/uniform-convergence and monotone-learning inputs, the proof still has to find a k-sized near-monotone decomposition without knowing the modes, control negligible intervals/heavy points and weighted aggregate error, and implement the novel tester using k-mode extremal witnesses. Complete semi-agnostic and confidence-tournament arguments are supplied rather than assumed. These intertwined algorithmic and statistical obligations yield one H2 k-modal learning outcome beyond ordinary empirical-distribution or concentration qualifications.",
  "source_scope_note": "Exact original Theorem1.1 only: learn an unknown k-modal distribution over [n] to total-variation accuracy epsilon and confidence1-delta with the exact stated near-optimal sample bound, succinct output and polynomial bit complexity. Full conservative introduction/model/learner context, atomic/superinterval/heavy-point construction, complete monotonicity tester and extremal witnesses, semi-agnostic Birge analysis, hypothesis tournament and confidence/running-time closing are retained. Standard VC/uniform-convergence and the named classical monotone learner are prerequisites; every source-local adaptation and tester argument is included. The simple learner, tester, constant-confidence version and helper claims remain uncounted support. Genuine already installed algorithm package is used; no algorithm2e acquisition/emulation. All-page source/output QA completed; exact final actual logs have zero missing-glyph/reference/citation/stability/box or other warning lines. Source-material candidate only, no admission/remote-delivery credit.",
  "source_quirks_note": "The full original algorithm retains Repeat Step3 at native1130, and the confidence-tournament proof retains total-variation threshold4delta at native2108. The source definition also literally calls accuracy epsilon/confidence delta while stating success1-delta. All original recipes and adjacent context remain unchanged; no step/parameter rewrite or mathematical-refereeing conclusion.",
